# Changelog

## v2.0.0

### Added

- `read_taco(path)` reads a TACO dataset. The default shape is one row per
  sample with one column per file of the sample's contract, named by the
  contract path and holding a GDAL `/vsisubfile/` pointer. `pivoted := false`
  gives one row per file with the metadata of every level it belongs to.
- All three TACO containers behind the same function: a `.zip` archive, a
  FOLDER directory, and a `.tacocat/` directory with consolidated metadata.
  TACOCAT results carry `source_file`, since sample ids restart per ZIP.
- A variable leaf, declared `prefix*[a,b].ext`, becomes one `LIST(VARCHAR)`
  column named after the prefix and ordered by the cardinal index.
- `idx` limits samples, `files` limits paths before the pivot, and `level`
  reads one metadata Parquet directly.
- `taco_contract()`, `taco_structure()`, `taco_levels()` and
  `taco_collection()` expose the contract without loading the json extension.
- `taco_sql()` returns the generated query, for debugging.
- A field name declared at two contract levels no longer collides. The row
  carries the value of the level it belongs to, and the shallower declaration
  is hidden, so `sample/before.resolution` wins over `sample.resolution` for a
  file under `before/`.
- Test fixtures for every shape the reader supports: flat, hierarchical,
  variable-leaf and null-structure archives, plus FOLDER and TACOCAT
  directories. `test/generate_taco_fixtures.py` rebuilds them.

### Changed

- `read_cozip` is now `read_flat`. The old name stays as a deprecated alias
  and will be removed in 3.0. Naming the readers after the profile they read
  makes the pair `read_flat` / `read_taco` obvious; `read_cozip` did not say
  which profile it handled, and a TACO archive is just as much a cozip.
- `read_flat` names the profile it found when handed the wrong one, and
  points at `read_taco`.
- The byte-0 index parser moved to `src/cozip_index.cpp` and now returns every
  priority entry from a single read, instead of looking up one fixed name. It
  also rejects entries whose byte range falls outside the archive, per cozip
  spec 7.5.
- `COLLECTION.json` is parsed with the yyjson that DuckDB already vendors and
  links, rather than a hand-written scanner.
- Test fixtures use the `.zip` extension, per cozip spec 13.4. The profile
  byte in the index is the authoritative signal; the file name never was.

### Removed

- WebAssembly builds. Browser users can use the JavaScript reader in the
  cozip repository.

### Notes

- TACO spec 8.2 calls the shape parameter `pivot`. DuckDB reserves that word
  for the `PIVOT` statement, so the reader spells it `pivoted`.
- The spec also describes `INSTALL taco FROM community`. Both readers ship in
  this one extension, because two extensions registering the same
  `cozip-subfile://` filesystem would collide when a user loads both.

## v1.3.0 — 2026-09-02

### Added

- `cozip_profile(path)` reads the 58-byte bootstrap and returns `none`,
  `flat`, `taco`, or `unknown:<value>` without opening a Parquet manifest.

### Changed

- The documented contract is explicitly Flat-only: `read_cozip()` rejects
  TACO-profile containers before attempting Parquet access.

## v1.2.0 — 2026-05-14

### Fixed
- WebAssembly (`wasm_eh`): `read_cozip(url)` returned memory corruption
  in `shell.duckdb.org`. Direct C++ construction of `ParquetReader` from
  cozip pulled template instantiations not exported by `duckdb-wasm` side
  modules; the dynamic linker resolved them to stubs returning 0, and the
  resulting null dereference leaked low linear memory into query results.
  Refactored `read_cozip` to a SQL table macro composing `read_parquet`
  over the existing `cozip-subfile://` VFS.

### Changed
- `read_cozip` is now a SQL table macro (was a C++ TableFunction). The
  user-facing API is unchanged: `SELECT * FROM read_cozip(p, gdal_vsi := ...)`.
- Geometry columns from GeoParquet sources arrive typed as `GEOMETRY` with
  CRS metadata, instead of raw `BLOB`. This follows `read_parquet`'s
  behaviour, which the new macro path goes through.
- `read_cozip(NULL)` now surfaces as a Parser Error mentioning
  `read_parquet`, since NULL propagates through the macro body before any
  cozip-level scalar runs (was a custom bind-time error).
- `parquet` is now a runtime dependency. Default DuckDB and `duckdb-wasm`
  builds autoload it transparently; embedded harnesses with autoload
  disabled must `LOAD parquet` explicitly.
